## 📁 Contents

| File | Algorithm | Time Complexity | Paradigm |
|---|---|---|---|
| `1_merge_sort.py` | Merge Sort + Inversion Counting | O(n log n) | Divide and Conquer |
| `2_randomized_quicksort.py` | Randomized QuickSort | O(n log n) expected | Randomized Algorithms |
| `3_karatsuba_multiplication.py` | Karatsuba Integer Multiplication | O(n^1.585) | Divide and Conquer |
| `4_fast_power.py` | Fast Exponentiation | O(log b) | Divide and Conquer |
| `5_randomized_selection.py` | Randomized Selection (QuickSelect) | O(n) expected | Randomized Algorithms |
| `6_master_theorem_analyzer.py` | Master Theorem Classifier | — | Recurrence Analysis (utility) |

## 🚀 Usage

Each script is self-contained and requires no external dependencies:

```bash
python3 1_merge_sort.py
python3 2_randomized_quicksort.py
python3 3_karatsuba_multiplication.py
python3 4_fast_power.py
python3 5_randomized_selection.py
python3 6_master_theorem_analyzer.py
```

## 📚 Topics Covered

- **Divide and Conquer**: Merge Sort, Karatsuba Multiplication, Fast Exponentiation, Master Theorem
- **Sorting and Searching**: Merge Sort, QuickSort, QuickSelect
- **Randomized Algorithms**: Randomized QuickSort, Randomized Selection, expected-time analysis

## ✅ Notes

Every script includes an `if __name__ == "__main__":` block with test cases that validate correctness against known-good references (e.g., Python's built-in `sorted()` or native multiplication) before printing results.

## 👤 About

This repository is part of my personal algorithms portfolio, developed while studying algorithm design and analysis independently and through structured coursework. It is intended to demonstrate practical understanding of algorithmic paradigms relevant to computer science graduate study.
